{"kind":"assert","text":"Hugh: label promised work accurately, so the board does not imply the whole queue is being worked on now.\n\nPromise #21571 explicitly places #21569 seventeenth in Claude's queue. The native commitment is promised, fresh and unreported. I passed those exact records through the current workRows function and rendered the actual RequestList component: the visible state is “in progress.” The [mapping](ui/src/lib/rows.ts@3ecb8b77d55cfe8ccb1d634649788a4ab629577b:176) derives that phrase from the presence of a promise; the [table](ui/src/components/RequestList.tsx@3ecb8b77d55cfe8ccb1d634649788a4ab629577b:295) displays it directly. The [probe](promised-queue-evidence.json) records both the queue statement and rendered result. This was a static component render, not an interactive browser exercise. The selector also calls its internal group running; that word is not a visible table heading.\n\nRecommendation: display “promised” for the durable promised state across table and graph. Keep the existing open counts, responsible actor, waiting-review/authorization/landing states, priority behavior and commitment identity. A promise records who owes the outcome, including work deliberately queued. Current activity belongs in the existing [leased attention display](ui/src/components/TopBar.tsx@3ecb8b77d55cfe8ccb1d634649788a4ab629577b:186), whose [rules](AGENTS.md@3ecb8b77d55cfe8ccb1d634649788a4ab629577b:151) already say that focus and busy status are advisory and expire. An expired or absent lease must not change the durable commitment or imply abandonment.\n\nThis makes a small presentation correction possible without adding queued/started events, forcing actors to withdraw and re-promise existing work, inventing queue positions from promise age, or requiring repetitive progress reports. Keep the accepted queue and delivery duties intact. If current activity is later shown beside a row, label it as session activity and preserve the lease boundary; it is not needed for this first repair.\n\nVerify the queued seventeenth example, an actively handled promise with identical durable facts, a promise with no current lease, and existing waiting/closed/stale rows through the actual table and graph. Their durable labels and population counts must remain independent of session presence. The benefit to test is whether Hugh can distinguish work owed from work currently receiving attention without opening every promise. No reduction in delivery time or communication volume has yet been measured.\n\nThis complements #21347's review-wait scheduling recommendation and #20910's shared lane classifier. It changes the wording of known facts; it does not propose a new lifecycle or alter either earlier recommendation. The [source bases](promised-queue-bases.json) are incorporated and byte-equal to current main. This note is for your review; no implementation or policy change is assigned here.","body":{"head":"3ecb8b77d55cfe8ccb1d634649788a4ab629577b","stale_bases":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:1fceb04f49f2e3869ec4936d6ab1fce1e7064b54, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:2b37ee0d6ca43aca851fa186221aa23e5475a842, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:6ddaa923c46e5c3a05b1c4c222eef77c453888a4, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:a1c15fb28f5df85b922f551195b7b736f2488138 stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:001b2e3b06105d22946682cb90201bc66c1c6a26, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:015aba51ab6c2416861618c5ac3c9ae0e444f17f, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:01c4e8b02601b3b35241c2baffd15e16e3e3d09d, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:02320b2eaa39a0ec7e47f31205c4b25afa46609f and 182 more"}}